          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.3 Relationship between Zeroes and Coefficients of a Polynomial"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.717,
            0.902,
            0.848
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have already seen that zero of a linear polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a x   +   b   \\mathrm { i s }   -   { \\frac { b } { - } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . We will now try to answer the question raised in Section 2.1 regarding the relationship between zeroes and coefficients of a quadratic polynomial. For this, let us take a quadratic polynomial, say "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x ) = 2 x ^ { 2 } - 8 x + 6 ."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . In Class IX, you have learnt how to factorise quadratic polynomials by splitting the middle term. So, here we need to split the middle term "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "- 8 x"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " as a sum of two terms, whose product is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "6 \\times 2 x ^ { 2 } = 1 2 x ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . So, we write"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.852,
            0.683,
            0.898
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_22.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}2x^{2}-8x+6&=2x^{2}-6x-2x+6=2x(x-3)-2(x-3)\\\\&=(2x-2)(x-3)=2(x-1)(x-3)\\end{aligned}"
        },
